;; Gate 4's ruleset — method §09's architecture-boundary check.
;;
;; Each namespace maps to the set of selfcheck namespaces it may require.
;; Anything required and not listed is a violation; anything outside the
;; selfcheck.* tree is ignored, because gate 4 is about YOUR layers, not your
;; dependencies. A namespace under src/ absent from this map is also a
;; violation — a new layer should be declared, not discovered.
;;
;; Two boundaries are being enforced here:
;;
;;   SIBLINGS — parse and compute may not see each other. Tests derived from an
;;   implementation only re-assert what the code already does.
;;
;;   THE PROTOCOL SEAM — compute depends on selfcheck.bindings, the protocol, and
;;   never on selfcheck.memory, an implementation of it. This is the one a
;;   Reviewer is told to look for by rule :review-scope.
;;
;; selfcheck.api is the composition root and is the only namespace allowed both
;; sides of either boundary.

{selfcheck.shapes   #{}
 selfcheck.bindings #{}
 selfcheck.expr     #{selfcheck.shapes}
 selfcheck.memory   #{selfcheck.bindings}
 selfcheck.parse    #{selfcheck.expr selfcheck.shapes}
 selfcheck.compute  #{selfcheck.expr selfcheck.shapes selfcheck.bindings}
 selfcheck.render   #{selfcheck.expr selfcheck.shapes}
 selfcheck.api      #{selfcheck.parse selfcheck.compute selfcheck.memory selfcheck.render}

 ;; A fixture, declared so the FORBIDDEN-DEPENDENCY path is exercised and not
 ;; only the undeclared one. red/seam.clj requires selfcheck.memory, which this
 ;; entry does not allow. See red/README.md. Named under :boundary/fixtures
 ;; because it is absent until `bb break seam` copies it in, and a declared
 ;; namespace with no file is otherwise an orphan - dead config - and fails.
 selfcheck.red-seam #{selfcheck.bindings}
 :boundary/fixtures #{selfcheck.red-seam}}
